{"causes": ["The assassination of Emperor Trebonianus Gallus and Volusianus by their own troops in 253 CE created a power vacuum within the Roman Empire.", "Aemilian's decisive victory over the Goths in Moesia in 253 CE led to his acclamation as emperor by his legions.", "Roman imperial tradition necessitated an Augusta to provide legitimacy and an appearance of dynastic continuity for newly proclaimed emperors."], "effects": ["Cornelia Supera's elevation briefly reinforced the traditional structure of the Roman imperial court during a period of extreme instability.", "Her ephemeral reign contributed to the numismatic record of the Crisis of the Third Century, providing valuable historical evidence for modern scholars.", "The brevity of her tenure as Augusta highlighted the precarious nature of imperial power and the rapid turnover of rulers in 253 CE."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "aemilian", "sourceName": "Aemilian", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "cornelia-supera", "targetName": "Cornelia Supera", "context": "Cornelia Supera served as empress consort to Emperor Aemilian during his brief reign in 253 CE, sharing the imperial dignity."}, {"sourceSlug": "crisis-of-the-third-century", "sourceName": "Crisis of the Third Century", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "cornelia-supera", "targetName": "Cornelia Supera", "context": "The short reign of Cornelia Supera as empress occurred directly within the turbulent political landscape of the Crisis of the Third Century (235-284 CE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "cornelia-supera", "sourceName": "Cornelia Supera", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "rome-italy", "targetName": "Rome, Italy", "context": "Cornelia Supera, as empress, was officially recognized and her imperial titles were proclaimed in Rome, the heart of the Roman Empire, in 253 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "numismatics", "sourceName": "Numismatics", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "cornelia-supera", "targetName": "Cornelia Supera", "context": "Numismatic evidence, specifically rare coins bearing her image, is the primary means by which Cornelia Supera's existence and imperial title are historically transmitted."}, {"sourceSlug": "aemilian", "sourceName": "Aemilian", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "cornelia-supera", "targetName": "Cornelia Supera", "context": "Aemilian's acclamation as Roman Emperor in 253 CE directly caused the elevation of his wife, Cornelia Supera, to the rank of Augusta."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Imperial Capital"}, {"name": "Moesia, Roman Empire", "role": "Husband's Proclamation Site"}, {"name": "Spoletium, Italy", "role": "Husband's Assassination Site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Cornelia Supera", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "cornelia-supera"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2275435", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornelia_Supera"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 4, "significanceNarrative": "Cornelia Supera's brief tenure as empress consort provides a small but crucial data point for understanding the extreme political instability and rapid imperial succession during the Roman Crisis of the Third Century, primarily through numismatic evidence. Her existence helps illustrate the traditional imperial mechanisms that persisted even amidst profound chaos.", "significanceCategory": "local"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-08T09:56:54Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28933759499", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Cornelia Supera (b. 200 CE) was a economist or social scientist of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Global. Known in the fields of monarchs & rulers.", "newValue": "Cornelia Supera, an empress consort of the Roman Empire during the tumultuous third century CE, held a brief but politically significant position alongside her husband, Emperor Aemilian. Flourishing around 253 CE, her presence at the apex of imperial power, even for a few months, reflects the rapid succession and instability characteristic of the Crisis of the Third Century, a period marked by civil war, economic collapse, and external invasions. Her role, though largely ceremonial, was integral to legitimizing Aemilian's short-lived reign, connecting his military ascendancy with the established traditions of imperial marriage and dynastic continuity in an era desperately seeking stability.

Her elevation to Augusta occurred in 253 CE, immediately following Aemilian's proclamation as emperor by his troops in Moesia after his decisive victory over the Goths led by King Cniva. While historical sources offer scant direct details about Cornelia Supera's personal actions or policies, her existence as empress is attested by numismatic evidence, specifically coins bearing her effigy with the title 'AUGUSTA.' These issues, though rare, underscore the imperial court's efforts to project an image of legitimate succession and shared authority, even as Aemilian's claim to the throne was contested by Valerian and Gallienus.

The swift downfall of Aemilian, who was assassinated by his own soldiers near Spoletium after only three months in power, naturally curtailed any potential legacy Cornelia Supera might have forged. Her brief tenure, however, serves as a poignant illustration of the precarious nature of imperial authority during the mid-third century. She represents one of many imperial consorts whose lives were inextricably linked to the fortunes of their husbands, reflecting the broader political instability that saw twenty-six emperors in fifty years."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-08T09:56:54Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28933759499", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "200 CE"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 CE"}], "newValue": ["The assassination of Emperor Trebonianus Gallus and Volusianus by their own troops in 253 CE created a power vacuum within the Roman Empire.", "Aemilian's decisive victory over the Goths in Moesia in 253 CE led to his acclamation as emperor by his legions.", "Roman imperial tradition necessitated an Augusta to provide legitimacy and an appearance of dynastic continuity for newly proclaimed emperors."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-08T09:56:54Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28933759499", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Cornelia Supera's elevation briefly reinforced the traditional structure of the Roman imperial court during a period of extreme instability.", "Her ephemeral reign contributed to the numismatic record of the Crisis of the Third Century, provi